! Silicon, one hundred and twenty-eight atoms: si8-1k.in stacked sixteen deep.
!
! Twice si64-1k.in -- 512 electrons, 256 bands -- in a tetragonal cell with
! c/a = 16, the same doubling rule that built si64 from si32 and si32 from si16.
! The largest cell in this directory, added 2026-08-26 to put a GPU against a
! serial Quantum ESPRESSO at a size where the comparison is worth making.
!
! **The energy per atom is not comparable across this ladder** -- doubling along
! z halves the zone, so one k-point of this cell folds two of si64's and a
! single-k run samples a different set of states. The cell is verified two ways
! that do not depend on sampling: every atom is 4-coordinated at exactly
! a*sqrt(3)/4, and si128 at Gamma reproduces si64 sampled at {Gamma, (0,0,1/2)}.
! See si32-1k.in for the same argument at a size where it is exact to 8.9e-16.
!
! Single k-point, like every other file here: both codes parallelise over k, so
! a multi-k comparison measures batching rather than the cost of the physics.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=6, celldm(1) =10.20, celldm(3) =16.0,
    nat=128, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
